<h1>Introduction to NGMS</h1>
<p class="page-meta">Start here — concepts and architecture before anything else.</p>

<h2>Introduction</h2>

<p>
  <strong>NGMS</strong> is a unified media management server built in
  Rust. Series, movies, indexers, and download management are handled by a single application —
  one database, one configuration file, one web interface — with a shared library, shared download
  queue, and integrated indexer search.
</p>
<p>
  It ships with <strong>embedded torrent and usenet download engines</strong>, so it can operate
  completely standalone: install it, point it at your media directories, add an indexer, and it
  will search, download, and organize your content automatically. External download clients
  (SABnzbd, qBittorrent, NZBGet) are also supported if you already run them.
</p>

<div class="callout callout-tip">
  <span class="callout-icon">✦</span>
  <div class="callout-body">
    <strong>Built in Rust.</strong> A single process, minimal memory footprint, no JVM, no Python
    interpreter, no Node runtime. The binary starts in under a second.
  </div>
</div>

<h2>Why unified?</h2>

<p>
  Managing a media library typically means running separate applications for series, movies, and
  indexers — each with its own process, database, configuration, and web interface. Keeping them
  synchronized means duplicating quality profiles, wiring API keys between services, and
  coordinating upgrades across independent codebases.
</p>

<p>
  NGMS is designed as a single application from the start. Every component shares one database,
  one configuration system, and one quality profile engine. There is nothing to keep in sync.
</p>

<div class="unified-box">
  <div class="ub-items">
    <div class="ub-item">📺 Series management</div>
    <div class="ub-item">🎬 Movie management</div>
    <div class="ub-item">🔍 Indexer search</div>
    <div class="ub-item">⬇️ Download engines</div>
    <div class="ub-item">📡 Plex integration</div>
    <div class="ub-item">🔔 Notifications</div>
  </div>
  <div class="ub-label">One process · One database · One config</div>
</div>

<h2>Core concepts</h2>

<p>
  Eight concepts underpin everything NGMS does. Understanding these before touching any settings
  will save you a lot of confusion.
</p>

<div class="concept-grid">

  <div class="concept-card">
    <div class="concept-icon" style="background:rgba(249,115,22,0.1);color:#f97316">📺</div>
    <h3>Series</h3>
    <p>
      A TV show you want NGMS to track. When you add a series, NGMS fetches all episode
      metadata from TMDB, and then monitors for episodes you don't have yet. You control which
      seasons and episodes are <strong>monitored</strong> — only monitored items are automatically
      searched.
    </p>
    <p class="concept-where">Found in: <code>Series</code> page</p>
  </div>

  <div class="concept-card">
    <div class="concept-icon" style="background:rgba(59,130,246,0.1);color:#3b82f6">🎬</div>
    <h3>Movie</h3>
    <p>
      A film you want NGMS to track. Behaves similarly to Series but without episode structure.
      NGMS watches for the release date and will search automatically once the movie is available
      on your preferred sources.
    </p>
    <p class="concept-where">Found in: <code>Movies</code> page</p>
  </div>

  <div class="concept-card">
    <div class="concept-icon" style="background:rgba(34,197,94,0.1);color:#22c55e">🔍</div>
    <h3>Indexer</h3>
    <p>
      A source NGMS queries to find releases — Newznab (usenet), Torznab (torrent), or a
      Cardigann YAML definition for custom sources. Indexers are configured once and shared
      across all searches for both series and movies.
    </p>
    <p class="concept-where">Found in: <code>Settings → Indexers</code></p>
  </div>

  <div class="concept-card">
    <div class="concept-icon" style="background:rgba(168,85,247,0.1);color:#a855f7">📦</div>
    <h3>Release</h3>
    <p>
      A specific file available for download, returned by an indexer search. A release has a
      name (e.g. <code>Show.S01E01.1080p.BluRay.x265</code>), size, categories, and a source
      URL or NZB file. NGMS evaluates each release against your quality profile before grabbing.
    </p>
    <p class="concept-where">Used in: <code>Queue</code>, <code>History</code></p>
  </div>

  <div class="concept-card">
    <div class="concept-icon" style="background:rgba(6,182,212,0.1);color:#06b6d4">⭐</div>
    <h3>Quality Profile</h3>
    <p>
      A named set of rules — which resolutions are acceptable, in what priority order, and when
      to stop upgrading. For example: "accept 1080p or better; upgrade from 720p if 1080p
      appears; never upgrade once Bluray is grabbed." Every series and movie is assigned one
      profile.
    </p>
    <p class="concept-where">Found in: <code>Settings → Quality Profiles</code></p>
  </div>

  <div class="concept-card">
    <div class="concept-icon" style="background:rgba(245,158,11,0.1);color:#f59e0b">⬇️</div>
    <h3>Download Client</h3>
    <p>
      Where files actually download to. NGMS supports <strong>embedded engines</strong>
      (built-in torrent via librtbit, built-in usenet via nzb-web) or <strong>external
      clients</strong> (SABnzbd, qBittorrent, NZBGet). You can configure multiple clients and
      assign categories or priorities.
    </p>
    <p class="concept-where">Found in: <code>Settings → Download Clients</code></p>
  </div>

  <div class="concept-card">
    <div class="concept-icon" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,0.1);color:#ef4444">📂</div>
    <h3>Import</h3>
    <p>
      The process of taking a completed download and moving (or hard-linking) it into your
      organized library. NGMS parses the filename, matches it to the correct series/movie/episode,
      applies your rename pattern, and places it in the right folder. Import can be automatic or
      manual.
    </p>
    <p class="concept-where">Found in: <code>Queue</code>, <code>Import</code> page</p>
  </div>

  <div class="concept-card">
    <div class="concept-icon" style="background:rgba(16,185,129,0.1);color:#10b981">🗂️</div>
    <h3>Library (Root Folder)</h3>
    <p>
      The root path where your organized media lives. You define separate root folders for series
      and movies, and NGMS places imported content there. Root folders must be accessible by the
      NGMS process — map Docker volumes accordingly.
    </p>
    <p class="concept-where">Found in: <code>Settings → Media Management</code></p>
  </div>

</div>

<h2>How it all fits together</h2>

<p>Every automated grab follows this path:</p>

<div class="flow-diagram">
  <div class="flow-step">
    <div class="flow-step-icon">🔍</div>
    <div class="flow-step-body">
      <strong>Search</strong>
      <span>Indexer queried (RSS or interactive)</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>
  <div class="flow-step">
    <div class="flow-step-icon">📋</div>
    <div class="flow-step-body">
      <strong>Release Evaluation</strong>
      <span>Parser extracts quality, language, episode info. Quality profile compared.</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>
  <div class="flow-step">
    <div class="flow-step-icon">⬇️</div>
    <div class="flow-step-body">
      <strong>Grab</strong>
      <span>Release sent to download client (embedded or external)</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>
  <div class="flow-step">
    <div class="flow-step-icon">✅</div>
    <div class="flow-step-body">
      <strong>Download Complete</strong>
      <span>Client signals completion; NGMS picks up the file</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>
  <div class="flow-step">
    <div class="flow-step-icon">📂</div>
    <div class="flow-step-body">
      <strong>Import</strong>
      <span>File renamed and moved to library root folder</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>
  <div class="flow-step">
    <div class="flow-step-icon">📡</div>
    <div class="flow-step-body">
      <strong>Plex Scan</strong>
      <span>Plex library section refreshed (if Plex integration enabled)</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>Embedded engines vs. external clients</h2>

<p>
  NGMS ships with two built-in download engines. You don't <em>have</em> to configure anything
  external — NGMS can operate completely standalone.
</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Mode</th>
      <th>How it works</th>
      <th>Best for</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Embedded torrent</strong></td>
      <td>librtbit runs inside the NGMS process. Torrents are tracked internally, ratio rules apply, seeding is managed by NGMS.</td>
      <td>New installs, simpler setups, single-binary deployments.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Embedded usenet</strong></td>
      <td>nzb-web runs inside the NGMS process. NZB files are downloaded, decoded (yEnc SIMD), PAR2 repaired, and extracted internally.</td>
      <td>New installs. Full pipeline in one process with no SABnzbd needed.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>External (SABnzbd, qBittorrent, etc.)</strong></td>
      <td>NGMS connects to the external client via its API. Downloads are managed by the external tool; NGMS only handles import when they complete.</td>
      <td>Existing setups, advanced per-client config, split hosts.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="callout callout-note">
  <span class="callout-icon">ℹ</span>
  <div class="callout-body">
    Both embedded and external clients can be active simultaneously. NGMS routes grabs based on
    protocol (usenet/torrent) and client priority order.
  </div>
</div>

<h2>Modules overview</h2>

<p>
  NGMS is modular — each section of the application can be enabled or disabled independently
  via <code>Settings → Modules</code>. Navigation items hide themselves when a module is disabled.
</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Module</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Series</td><td>TV show library — track, search, and organize episodes automatically</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Movies</td><td>Film library — monitor releases, quality upgrades, import</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Torrent</td><td>Built-in torrent client UI (librtbit engine)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Usenet</td><td>Built-in usenet client UI (nzb-web engine)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Search</td><td>Interactive search across all configured indexers</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Discover</td><td>Browse trending and popular content via TMDB</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Calendar</td><td>Upcoming episode and movie release schedule</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Streaming</td><td>Direct play and HLS transcode via ffmpeg</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Plex</td><td>Plex API integration — library scans, watchlist sync, activity view</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Requests</td><td>Multi-user request and approval workflow</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Notifications</td><td>Discord, Telegram, Slack, email, webhook alerting</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

